\section{Bottleneck experiment details}
\label{app:bottleneck-family}

\paragraph{Process and policies.}
\label{app:bottleneck-family-gen}
All experiments use the nine-state transition graph in \cref{fig:didactic-mdp}, with two deterministic actions per state, $\gamma=0.8$, and a uniform reference distribution
$\rho(s^+)=1/9$.
Entering A (state $3$) gives reward $1$, every tail state ($5$--$8$) gives $0.5$, and all other states give $0$.
The main example, P9, uses a continuation policy that chooses each action with probability $1/2$.
For five additional settings, R1--R5, we keep the transitions fixed and independently draw the probability of the first action at each state from $\mathcal U(0.25,0.75)$.
The sampling procedure retains a draw only if the true action-value difference is at least $0.5$ and the best rank-$2$ linear fit retains less than half of it.
All five initial draws passed these conditions.
Because every tail state carries the same reward, the value of the tail does not depend on the policy, so the true action-value difference at state $2$ is exactly $1$ in all six settings.
\Cref{fig:family-graphs} lists the six policy settings.

